# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sanity — WEF Canada Blog (project pvj2zu1w)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# All project updates, success stories and active-project pages live in Sanity.
# `npm run build` runs scripts/sanity-sync.mjs first, which renders them into
# pages/updates, pages/stories and pages/projects. Set these three in the host's
# build environment so a deploy picks up the latest published content.
SANITY_PROJECT_ID="pvj2zu1w"
SANITY_DATASET="production"
SANITY_API_VERSION="2026-07-08"

# REQUIRED AT BUILD TIME. Posts, authors and categories in this project are not
# publicly readable: an unauthenticated query succeeds but returns nothing, so a
# build with no token silently has no content and fails. A **Viewer** token is
# enough — a build environment never needs write access. Image assets are public,
# so cdn.sanity.io images on the live site work without any token.
#
# Server-side only. Deliberately no VITE_ prefix, so Vite can never inline it
# into the browser bundle. Create one at:
#   https://www.sanity.io/manage/project/pvj2zu1w/api#tokens
SANITY_API_TOKEN=""

# Supabase — read at BUILD time by vite.config.js and inlined into the bundle,
# which is why these two carry no VITE_ prefix: Hostinger injects them under
# these exact names once the Supabase database is linked to the app, so there
# is nothing to set there by hand. Copy them into a local .env to test forms.
#
# SUPABASE_API_KEY must be the PUBLIC anon key (Supabase dashboard →
# Project Settings → API → "anon public"). Never put the service_role key here:
# anything in these two variables ships to the browser. Row Level Security in
# supabase/schema.sql is what keeps the data safe.
SUPABASE_URL="https://your-project-ref.supabase.co"
SUPABASE_API_KEY="your-anon-public-key"
